course: dictionary
lesson: 15265
title: Short forms in casual talk
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words get shortened when people talk or text each other. Sib is a short form of sibling, and both mean a brother or sister. «I went to the park with my sib after school.» The word my tells you the sib is one of your family, and after school tells you it is a casual, everyday sentence.
explain: Sibs is the plural, so it means more than one. «My sibs are older than me, so they get to stay up late.» The sibs here are brothers and sisters together. If you see the word sibs, ask yourself: am I talking about more than one brother or sister?
explain: Sib is informal slang, so you will see it in texts and chatty talk, not in school essays or formal letters. In a formal letter, write sibling or brother or sister instead. An old story might use sib to mean a relative, a member of the family, so read the clues around it to check which meaning fits.
fact: Sib is a short form of sibling.
fact: A sibling is a brother or sister.
fact: «My sib» means one of your brothers or sisters.
fact: «My sibs» means your brothers and sisters together.
fact: In old writing, sib can mean a relative, which is any member of your family.
exercise: «My sibs are coming to the park with me.» What does sibs mean here? | Shoes | Parents | Friends from school | *Brothers and sisters
exercise: Maya texts, «my sib is so annoying lol». Who is Maya's sib? | A neighbour | *A brother or sister | Her dog | Her teacher
exercise: Which word is a short form of sibling? | *sib | sit | sign | slip
test: «The sibs ran down the stairs together.» What does the sibs mean here? | The parents | One brother only | The school bus driver | *Brothers and sisters together
test: In an old tale, «The queen wrote to her sib across the sea.» In this old use, what might sib mean? | *A relative, a member of the family | A ship's captain | A pet cat | A teacher at school
exam: Which word should you use in a formal letter to your head teacher? | Sis | Sib | Sibs | *Sibling
